    51	## 3. The split, and why it was not a contradiction
    52	
    53	codex filed `EXCHANGE_NEGLIGIBLE`. It computed a **different object**:
    54	
    55	- **Object L** — `⟨Σ_a s_a(x) s_a(x)⟩` at coincident `x`: square, then coarse-grain. The exchange line is unconstrained
    56	  by a spatial delta function, so it is a product of two momentum integrals and scales as `n²`.
    57	- **Object C** — `⟨S_a S_a⟩/V²` with `S_a = ∫_V s_a`: coarse-grain, then square. The spatial integrals force equal
    58	  momentum modes, collapsing the double integral to a single one, so everything scales as `n/V` and vanishes in the
    59	  thermodynamic limit. This is K3 step 1's object.
    60	
    61	Route 2 computed **both**, blind, by a different method, and reproduced both: Object L `→ −3N_f(c₁²+c₂²)`, Object C
    62	`→ 0` as `V → ∞`. The two route-1 seats were never in contradiction about one quantity.
    63	
    64	**Which object the field equations require.** `U^ik` (entry 10 Eq. (6), L84–86) is a local algebraic function of the
    65	spin tensor, carried into Eq. (9) (L104–106); the Einstein–Cartan equations are local, so the source term is the
    66	expectation of the local product — **Object L**. Route 2 adds the decisive check: on Object C the unpolarized mean spin
    67	is zero, so squaring after averaging would annihilate the term entirely, and the paper plainly does not intend that.
    68	**But the source text does not say.** L108–110 says only that the first term of Eq. (9) "can be macroscopically averaged
    69	at cosmological scales as a perfect fluid" and L113 then asserts `⟨s²⟩ = ¾ n²` without defining the average, the
    70	ordering, or the contractions. The physics fixes the object; the paper does not.
