arcia de Andrade, Nuovo Cim. B 116 , 1107 (2001). [35] E. Weber, J. Math. Phys. 25 , 3279 (1984); J. D. Barrow, Phys. Rev. D 51 , 3113 (1995); M. P. Da̧browski, Astrophys. Space Sci. 240 , 123 (1996); P. Borgohain and M. Patgiri, Indian J. Phys. A 72 , 331 (1998). [36] M. Demiański, Nature 307 , 140 (1984); A. B. Burd and J. D. Barrow, Nucl. Phys. B 308 , 929 (1988); Nucl. Phys. B 324 , 276 (1989); J. P. de León, J. Math. Phys. 31 , 371 (1990); L. E. Mendes and A. B. Henriques, Phys. Lett. B 254 , 44 (1991); S. Byland and D. Scialom, Phys. Rev. D 57 , 6065 (1998); S. D. Katore and R. S. Rane, Astrophys. Space Sci. 323 , 293 (2009). [37] X.-Z. Li and J.-G. Hao, Phys. Rev. D 68 , 083512 (2003). [38] J. D. Barrow and C. G. Tsagas, Class. Quantum Grav. 22 , 1563 (2005). [39] D. Solomons, P. K. S. Dunsby, and G. F. R. Ellis, Class. Quantum Grav. 23 , 6585 (2006). [40] W. Kopczyński, Phys. Lett. A 43 , 63 (1973). [41] B. Kuchowicz, J. Phys. A: Math. Gen. 8 , L29 (1975). [42] J. Tafel, Phys. Lett. A 45 , 341 (1973). [43] D. Tsoubelis, Phys. Rev. D 20 , 3004 (1979); N. A. Batakis and D. Tsoubelis, Phys. Rev. D 26 , 2611 (1982); D. Lorenz, Gen. Relativ. Gravit. 14 , 691 (1982). [44] B. Kuchowicz, Phys. Lett. A 54 , 13 (1975); Astrophys. Space Sci. 39 , 157 (1976); Astrophys. Space Sci. 40 , 167 (1976); Acta Phys. Pol. B 7 , 81 (1976). [45] D. Lorenz, J. Phys. A: Math. Gen. 15 , 2809 (1982). [46] L. Parker, Phys. Rev. Lett. 21 , 562 (1968); Phys. Rev. 183 , 1057 (1969); Y. B. Zel’dovich, J. Exp. Theor. Phys. Lett. 12 , 307 (1970); L. Parker, Phys. Rev. D 3 , 346 (1971); Phys. Rev. D 3 , 2546 (1971); Y. B. Zel’dovich and A. A. Starobinskii, J. Exp. Theor. Phys. Lett. 26 , 252 (1977); V. A. Beilin, G. M. Vereshkov, Y. S. Grishkan, N. M. Ivanov, V. A. Nesterenko, and A. N. Poltavtsev, J. Exp. Theor. Phys. 51 , 1045 (1980). [47] R. P. Kerr, Phys. Rev. Lett. 11 , 237 (1963). [48] D. Saadeh, S. M. Feeney, A. Pontzen, H. V. Peiris, and J. D. McEwen, Phys. Rev. Lett. 117 , 131302 (2016); J. Colin, R. Mohayaee, M. Rameez, and S. Sarkar, Astron. Astrophys. Lett. 631 , L13 (2019); K. Migkas, G. Schellenberger, T. H. Reiprich, F. Pacaud, M. E. Ramos-Ceja, and L. Lovisari, Astron. Astrophys. 636 , A15 (2020). Experimental support, please view the build logs for errors. Generated by L A T E xml . Instructions for reporting errors We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile\n      support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the\n      methods listed below: Click the \"Report Issue\" ( ) button, located in the page header. Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report. Our team has already identified the following issues . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we\n      may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability\n      should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for\n      all. Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a list of packages that need conversion , and welcome developer contributions . We gratefully acknowledge support from\n        our major funders , member institutions , ,\n        and all contributors. About · Help · Contact · Subscribe · Copyright · Privacy · Accessibility · Operational Status (opens in new tab) Major funding support from"}
{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.11556", "final_url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.11556", "status": 200, "bytes": 42813, "sha256": "4d4e23c9c11aaf8afd62f57059ca0de96d84e3eebc783502a5160595bbd65146", "text": "[2007.11556] A nonsingular, anisotropic universe in a black hole with torsion and particle production Skip to main content Search Submit Donate Log in Search arXiv Press Enter to search · Advanced search General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology arXiv:2007.11556 (gr-qc) [Submitted on 22 Jul 2020 ( v1 ), last revised 23 Jun 2026 (this version, v2)] Title: A nonsingular, anisotropic universe in a black hole with torsion and particle production Authors: Nikodem J. Popławski View a PDF of the paper titled A nonsingular, anisotropic universe in a black hole with torsion and particle production, by Nikodem J. Pop{\\l}awski View PDF HTML (experimental) Abstract: We consider a universe formed in a black hole in general relativity with spin and torsion. The interior of a Schwarzschild black hole can be represented by the Kantowski$-$Sachs metric that describes a closed anisotropic universe. We use this metric to derive the Einstein$-$Cartan field equations with a relativistic spin fluid as a source. We show that torsion may prevent a singularity and replace it with a nonsingular bounce if particle production dominates over shear. Particle production after the last bounce can generate a finite period of inflation, during which the universe expands and isotropizes to the current state. This scenario is only approximate: the Kantowski$-$Sachs metric is never reached and should be replaced with a more general metric that tends to that of a 3-sphere. Comments: 9 pages; published version Subjects: General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc) ; Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO) Cite as: arXiv:2007.11556 [gr-qc] (or arXiv:2007.11556v2 [gr-qc] for this version) https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2007.11556 Focus to learn more arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite Jou